WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.300
An ABI safety roadmap starts by identifying binary boundaries deliberately.

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:10.600
Decide which interfaces are stable commitments and which are internal details.

3
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:15.100
Keep stable binary interfaces narrow, versioned, and documented.

4
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:29.600
Hide implementation layout where practical, and avoid exposing fragile C++ details such as templates, inline implementation, standard library types, and virtual hierarchies when long-term compatibility is required.

5
00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:45.160
Use patterns that make evolution predictable: opaque handles, C-compatible facades, PIMPL, versioned structs, capability queries, explicit ownership rules, paired allocation and release functions, and clear error translation.

6
00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:48.460
Control symbol visibility and dependency assumptions.

7
00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:58.400
Treat plugins and third-party modules as both compatibility boundaries and potential trust boundaries, with clear lifecycle, threading, ownership, and shutdown rules.

8
00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.920
Finally, verify the promise.

9
00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:04.140
Test old clients against new libraries where practical.

10
00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:07.920
Inspect exported symbols and release artifacts.

11
00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:11.380
Track dependency and runtime changes.

12
00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:17.240
Document supported platforms, version policy, migration paths, and compatibility impact.

13
00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:27.100
ABI stability supports both reliability and security because it keeps upgrades predictable, resource ownership clear, and binary integration behavior reviewable over time.

14
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:33.280
Treat every release as an opportunity to confirm that the binary contract still matches the documented contract.

15
00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.840
The habit to carry forward is intentionality.

16
00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:43.340
Do not let ABI promises emerge accidentally from whatever symbols happen to be visible or whatever types happen to appear in a header.

17
00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:53.600
Choose the boundary, document it, keep it narrow, test it as a product feature, and change it only with the same care you would apply to any security-relevant interface.
